{"st":"MO","name":"Missouri","seats":[{"census":1820,"seats":1},{"census":1830,"seats":2},{"census":1840,"seats":5},{"census":1850,"seats":7},{"census":1860,"seats":9},{"census":1870,"seats":13},{"census":1880,"seats":14},{"census":1890,"seats":15},{"census":1900,"seats":16},{"census":1910,"seats":16},{"census":1920,"seats":16},{"census":1930,"seats":13},{"census":1940,"seats":13},{"census":1950,"seats":11},{"census":1960,"seats":10},{"census":1970,"seats":10},{"census":1980,"seats":9},{"census":1990,"seats":9},{"census":2000,"seats":9},{"census":2010,"seats":8},{"census":2020,"seats":8}],"events":[{"congress":17,"year":1821,"kind":"statehood","title":"Statehood","text":"Missouri became the 24th state on August 10, 1821.","sources":["https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/R47747.html"]},{"congress":23,"year":1832,"kind":"census","title":"1830 census apportionment","text":"After the 1830 census, Missouri's House delegation grew from 1 to 2 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":23,"year":1832,"kind":"at-large","title":"Statewide (general ticket) elections","text":"In the 23rd through 29th Congresses (1833–1847), Missouri elected all of its representatives statewide on a general ticket instead of by district. This continued after the Apportionment Act of 1842 required districts, and the House seated the members anyway.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/","https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/IF12567.html"]},{"congress":28,"year":1842,"kind":"census","title":"1840 census apportionment","text":"After the 1840 census, Missouri's House delegation grew from 2 to 5 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":33,"year":1852,"kind":"census","title":"1850 census apportionment","text":"After the 1850 census, Missouri's House delegation grew from 5 to 7 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":38,"year":1862,"kind":"census","title":"1860 census apportionment","text":"After the 1860 census, Missouri's House delegation grew from 7 to 9 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":43,"year":1872,"kind":"census","title":"1870 census apportionment","text":"After the 1870 census, Missouri's House delegation grew from 9 to 13 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":48,"year":1882,"kind":"census","title":"1880 census apportionment","text":"After the 1880 census, Missouri's House delegation grew from 13 to 14 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":53,"year":1892,"kind":"census","title":"1890 census apportionment","text":"After the 1890 census, Missouri's House delegation grew from 14 to 15 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":58,"year":1902,"kind":"census","title":"1900 census apportionment","text":"After the 1900 census, Missouri's House delegation grew from 15 to 16 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":63,"year":1912,"kind":"census","title":"1910 census apportionment","text":"After the 1910 census, Missouri kept 16 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":73,"year":1932,"kind":"census","title":"1930 census apportionment","text":"After the 1930 census, Missouri's House delegation shrank from 16 to 13 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":73,"year":1932,"kind":"at-large","title":"Statewide (general ticket) elections","text":"In the 73rd Congress (1933–1935), Missouri elected all of its representatives statewide on a general ticket instead of by district. The 1929 apportionment law had no districting rules, so states without a new map after losing or gaining seats could elect members at-large.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/","https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/IF12567.html"]},{"congress":78,"year":1942,"kind":"census","title":"1940 census apportionment","text":"After the 1940 census, Missouri kept 13 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":83,"year":1952,"kind":"census","title":"1950 census apportionment","text":"After the 1950 census, Missouri's House delegation shrank from 13 to 11 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":88,"year":1962,"kind":"census","title":"1960 census apportionment","text":"After the 1960 census, Missouri's House delegation shrank from 11 to 10 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":91,"year":1969,"kind":"court","title":"Kirkpatrick v. Preisler","text":"The Supreme Court struck down Missouri's congressional map, holding that even small population differences between districts must be justified; there is no fixed amount that is automatically acceptable.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/394/526"]},{"congress":93,"year":1972,"kind":"census","title":"1970 census apportionment","text":"After the 1970 census, Missouri kept 10 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":98,"year":1982,"kind":"census","title":"1980 census apportionment","text":"After the 1980 census, Missouri's House delegation shrank from 10 to 9 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":103,"year":1992,"kind":"census","title":"1990 census apportionment","text":"After the 1990 census, Missouri kept 9 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":108,"year":2002,"kind":"census","title":"2000 census apportionment","text":"After the 2000 census, Missouri kept 9 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":113,"year":2012,"kind":"census","title":"2010 census apportionment","text":"After the 2010 census, Missouri's House delegation shrank from 9 to 8 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":118,"year":2022,"kind":"census","title":"2020 census apportionment","text":"After the 2020 census, Missouri kept 8 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":119,"year":2025,"kind":"mid-decade","title":"Mid-decade redistricting and referendum","text":"In September 2025 the legislature passed a new congressional map in a special session. Opponents gathered signatures for a referendum, and after court rulings the 2022 map was used in the November 2026 election.","sources":["https://pbs.org/newshour/politics/supreme-court-rejects-missouris-push-to-use-new-republican-drawn-u-s-house-map-in-november","https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/missouri/"]},{"congress":119,"year":2026,"kind":"other","title":"Voters reject the 2025 map","text":"On November 3, 2026 voters rejected Proposition A, the referendum on the 2025 map, according to unofficial results, so the 2022 map stays in place.","sources":["https://stlpr.org/government-politics-issues/2026-11-03/missouri-voters-reject-proposition-a-congressional-redistricting-map"]}],"compactness":{"c017":{"MO-0":0.485},"c018":{"MO-0":0.485},"c019":{"MO-0":0.485},"c020":{"MO-0":0.485},"c021":{"MO-0":0.485},"c022":{"MO-0":0.408},"c023":{"MO-0":0.408},"c024":{"MO-0":0.408},"c025":{"MO-0":0.408},"c026":{"MO-0":0.408},"c027":{"MO-0":0.408},"c028":{"MO-0":0.408},"c029":{"MO-0":0.408},"c030":{"MO-1":0.272,"MO-2":0.32,"MO-3":0.227,"MO-4":0.35,"MO-5":0.451},"c031":{"MO-1":0.272,"MO-2":0.32,"MO-3":0.227,"MO-4":0.35,"MO-5":0.451},"c032":{"MO-1":0.272,"MO-2":0.32,"MO-3":0.227,"MO-4":0.35,"MO-5":0.451},"c033":{"MO-1":0.437,"MO-2":0.447,"MO-3":0.474,"MO-4":0.425,"MO-5":0.372,"MO-6":0.402,"MO-7":0.341},"c034":{"MO-1":0.437,"MO-2":0.447,"MO-3":0.474,"MO-4":0.425,"MO-5":0.372,"MO-6":0.402,"MO-7":0.341},"c035":{"MO-1":0.437,"MO-2":0.447,"MO-3":0.474,"MO-4":0.425,"MO-5":0.372,"MO-6":0.402,"MO-7":0.341},"c036":{"MO-1":0.437,"MO-2":0.447,"MO-3":0.474,"MO-4":0.425,"MO-5":0.372,"MO-6":0.402,"MO-7":0.341},"c037":{"MO-1":0.437,"MO-2